WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.320
It is known that in Game of Thrones "all men
must die", but even on a show that's renowned

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.230
for some of the bloodiest, most shocking deaths
on TV, from the Red Wedding to the massacre

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:14.389
at the Great Sept of Baelor, apparently some
things are still just too terrible to be seen.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.160
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video I'll be revealing brutal

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.980
Game of Thrones deaths that ended up censored
or cut from the epic fantasy series.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.390
And my Game of Thrones Funko Pop giveaway
is running on this video too, so for a chance

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.470
to win: be sure to subscribe to my channel
and leave a comment with your thoughts about

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.470
the show.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.420
Quick warning: spoilers ahead if you're not
caught up with Game of Thrones.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.350
Given Ramsay Bolton's penchant for hunting
down humans with his hounds, his death at

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.950
the paws of his own pups in season 6 was a
fitting end to his horrifically sadistic story.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.310
But the Bolton boy's death by dog in episode
9, "Battle of the Bastards", was initially

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.740
going to be much gorier than it ended up.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.790
In the actual episode, apart from the hounds'
first bites to Ramsay's face and a shot of

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.900
his bloody hand, much of Ramsay's demise plays
out off-screen with the camera focusing on

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.850
Sansa's face as we hear the grisly sound of
her tormentor dying.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:10.320
However, according to an interview in Variety
with visual effects supervisor Mat Krentz,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.430
a computer-generated jaw was also created
for Ramsay, which was then animated to show

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.910
the flesh being ripped from his face.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.760
In the end though, those effects were deemed
too grisly and were cut.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.220
But that change also had an interesting effect
on the overall scene, because if Ramsay's

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.600
on-screen fate had been more graphic, I think
we would have lost some of the emphasis on

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.150
Sansa and how her character has been changed
by her traumatic experiences, which was a

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.470
crucial part of this scene that we see in
how she reacts to her persecutor finally getting

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.780
his comeuppance.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.130
In Game of Thrones, brutal deaths come to
many men, both bad and good, and season 6

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:49.280
gave us a heart-breaking scene in the episode
"The Door" where Hodor sacrificed himself

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.080
to save Bran and Meera.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.460
In a time-looped scene, which also revealed
the origin of Hodor's name, the gentle giant

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:59.690
held the door, helping Bran and Meera to escape
the army of the dead that had invaded the

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.670
cave of The Three-Eyed Raven.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.830
Hodor used all his strength to keep the wights
in the cave, though we do see them begin to

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.760
break through and claw at him.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.530
So, while we don't actually see Hodor's death
on screen, it's highly unlikely he survived

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.450
unless he was turned into a wight himself.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.050
Originally, however, the episode's director
Jack Bender did want to show Hodor's brutal

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.210
death on screen.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.110
In an interview with Observer.com, Bender
explained that "what the dead would be doing

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.390
to Hodor would be ripping his clothes off
once they got through that door.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.050
They would be ripping his flesh off.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:34.410
[And] if the dead can go through wood, they're
going to be tearing Hodor apart."

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:39.200
Showrunners Benioff and Weiss though suggested
that showing Hodor die in that way would be

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.620
"too horrific" and therefore the audience
might not fully "feel the loss of" the character.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.470
In the end, Bender toned down the horror of
the scene, including just the right amount

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:51.890
of scary so that we see "Hodor surrounded
and engulfed by [these] skeletal arms and

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:53.160
long fingers."

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.170
That seems like a good choice as the scene
was already distressing enough as it took

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:01.890
away a fan favourite just as we discovered
the truth about his origin.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:07.090
Cersei Lannister is certainly not a character
you want to cross if you'd like to continue

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:12.290
living, and Septa Unella found that out to
her cost in the final episode of season 6.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:17.000
And, boy, did Cersei relish the opportunity
to turn the tables on the woman who led her

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.550
on her walk of shame.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:23.420
Yes, Cersei really dragged out Unella's fate,
starting off with a bit of wineboarding, followed

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.390
by a monologue to ram home the fact that she
fully understood the power trip Unella had

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.140
enjoyed while mistreating her.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.910
And, as the door closed on Unella's cell,
we were left with the sound of the Septa's

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.810
screams as Gregor Clegane began Cersei's long
and painful revenge.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.050
But disturbing as the thought and sound of
Septa Unella's fate was, Lena Headey told

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:46.950
EW that the scene we saw in the show was just
the "tame version" and it was actually "meant

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.860
to be worse, but [in the end] they couldn't
do it."

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.769
Game of Thrones loves to give us a good old
kick in the gut by killing off in horrific

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:57.019
ways characters we've been rooting for, the
Red Wedding being a classic example.

00:03:57.019 --> 00:04:01.360
And it was another fan favourite, Oberyn Martell,
who suffered perhaps one of the goriest deaths

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.780
on the show so far.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:07.280
It was such a cruel moment when Oberyn, after
almost getting revenge for the rape and murder

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.760
of his sister Elia and her children, was tripped
up by The Mountain who proceeded to use his

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.860
thumbs to poke out Oberyn's eyes and crush
his head.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:19.759
As appalling as the final version we saw in
the series was, the showrunners actually cut

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:22.749
a significant amount of gore from that scene.

00:04:22.749 --> 00:04:26.669
According to actor Ben Crompton, who plays
Dolorous Edd, there was an edit of the scene

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:32.210
that showed the Mountain basically pull apart
all of Oberyn's face in full graphic detail.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.779
In the scene we see in the show, the camera
cuts to a shot of The Mountain just after

00:04:35.779 --> 00:04:39.849
he says, "Then I smashed her head in like
this", and we hear the sound of Oberyn's head

00:04:39.849 --> 00:04:40.849
imploding.

00:04:40.849 --> 00:04:44.810
I think many fans were probably relieved that
we didn't get to see the even more shocking

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:49.169
edit as the combination of seeing Oberyn's
eyes gouged out combined with the crunching

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:52.960
sound that follows was more than enough!

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.150
Ellaria Sand took revenge on the Lannisters
for Oberyn's death a season later when she

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.389
poisoned Myrcella with a fatal kiss on the
lips just before the young princess left Dorne

00:05:01.389 --> 00:05:03.990
to travel back to King's Landing with Jaime.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:09.029
In an interview at MCM Comic Con, Nell Tiger
Free who was recast as Myrcella in Season

00:05:09.029 --> 00:05:12.979
5, revealed that a much bloodier version of
her death was initially planned.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:17.039
Free explained that she was given "mashed-up
bananas with [...] fake blood, and [her] brains

00:05:17.039 --> 00:05:19.210
were supposed to be all over the ship."

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:23.730
It seems unlikely the poison would have directly
caused Myrcella's brains to spill out, but

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.690
it's possible it would have caused her to
fall and split open her head.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:32.290
In the end though, the producers decided against
that more gruesome death as they wanted Myrcella's

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:35.199
death to reflect her sweet character.

00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:39.620
We've seen this season how crucial Dany's
dragons are to her attempts at taking the

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.169
Iron Throne, but back in season 2 she almost
lost them in the city of Qarth.

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:48.689
In the sixth episode of that season, she returned
to her quarters in the city to find her guards

00:05:48.689 --> 00:05:52.729
massacred, her handmaiden Irri dead, and her
young dragons stolen.

00:05:52.729 --> 00:05:57.340
We discover later that Dany's other handmaiden
Doreah conspired to betray her, and there's

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:01.159
a deleted scene in the extras for Season 2
which shows that it was Doreah herself who

00:06:01.159 --> 00:06:03.249
treacherously killed Irri.

00:06:03.249 --> 00:06:07.650
In an interview with Winter Is Coming, actress
Amrita Acharia, who played Irri, explained

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:12.159
that in order to make her death look realistic,
she was actually strangled a bit during filming

00:06:12.159 --> 00:06:15.580
which left her with huge bruises on her neck
the following day.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.499
I'm guessing what hurt even more than shooting
her death scene though was finding out, after

00:06:19.499 --> 00:06:23.779
all that, that it had actually been given
the chop from the final show!

00:06:23.779 --> 00:06:28.409
Another death we never got to see on screen
was when Brienne of Tarth executed Stannis

00:06:28.409 --> 00:06:33.129
Baratheon in the season 5 finale; however,
originally there was an extended version of

00:06:33.129 --> 00:06:34.809
this scene in the script.

00:06:34.809 --> 00:06:38.889
The scene we did see was relatively short
and simple with Brienne finding Stannis after

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:40.960
his army has been decimated by the Boltons.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.979
After Brienne confronts Stannis about Renly
and he confesses, she sentences him to die

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:45.539
but gives him a chance for some last words.

00:06:45.539 --> 00:06:50.139
In the episode which aired, Stannis replies
in typical Stannis fashion, "Go on.

00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:51.339
Do your duty."

00:06:51.339 --> 00:06:55.469
But it's just before this line that the original
script included a small redemptive moment

00:06:55.469 --> 00:06:58.819
for the man who believed he was the rightful
king of Westeros.

00:06:58.819 --> 00:07:02.990
In the extended scene, Stannis shares that
he doesn't really believe in the afterlife,

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:06.919
even though he'd converted to the religion
of the Lord of Light under the influence of

00:07:06.919 --> 00:07:07.919
Melisandre.

00:07:07.919 --> 00:07:11.389
He also confesses that he's sorry for what
he did to his brother Renly and struggles

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:15.720
with emotion as he thinks of his daughter
Shireen who he burned alive in the previous

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:16.720
episode.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.000
This final moment would have been a very rare
display of emotion and regret for Stannis

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.979
and, although there were hints in the show
that he'd been shaken by his sacrifice of

00:07:24.979 --> 00:07:28.399
Shireen, this could have been an interesting
addition to Stannis' death scene, showing

00:07:28.399 --> 00:07:30.199
another side of his character.

00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:33.719
In the end, it's not clear why the showrunners
cut the moment from the episode, but it's

00:07:33.719 --> 00:07:38.990
likely they felt the final cut was more true
to Stannis's stoic, unflinching character.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.849
In fact, deleted death scenes go all the way
back to the pilot where we would have seen

00:07:43.849 --> 00:07:44.849
the death of Jon Arryn.

00:07:44.849 --> 00:07:49.069
Remember, it was Jon Arryn's death by poison
which kicked off the whole chain of events

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:53.159
in the series when Catelyn Stark received
a letter from her sister Lysa, who was also

00:07:53.159 --> 00:07:57.429
Jon's wife, saying that the Lannisters had
murdered her husband, the then hand of the

00:07:57.429 --> 00:08:00.839
King, and that they were now conspiring against
King Robert Baratheon.

00:08:00.839 --> 00:08:04.740
John Standing, the actor who played Arryn,
revealed in an interview that he filmed a

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.900
scene which began with him on his death bed
dying from poisoning and continued with him

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:13.139
crawling across the room to try to write a
message, but when he reached for the ink,

00:08:13.139 --> 00:08:14.999
it came crashing down on him.

00:08:14.999 --> 00:08:18.830
Lena Headey's Cersei then entered the room
and stamped on his fingers before he could

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:23.509
write anything, after which he finally succumbed
to the poison.

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:27.709
Standing's other scene, where his corpse lay
in state in the Red Keep, did however make

00:08:27.709 --> 00:08:32.140
it into the first episode and, incredibly,
the actor has said he actually receives fan

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.050
mail for that tiny motionless, non-speaking
role!

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:38.890
I suspect this death scene was cut because
it would have implied that Cersei definitely

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:43.220
knew about Jon Arryn's suspicions of her incestuous
relationship with her brother, and so she

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:44.240
had him killed.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.330
Instead, it's a much more interesting and
mysterious set-up for the series to leave

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.350
the exact reasons for Jon Arryn's death uncertain.

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:55.930
The deleted scene would also have made Cersei
into more of a stereotyped evil villain too

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:57.220
early in the show.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:01.390
Game of Thrones loves to play with ambiguity
and tease us over a long period, and, let's

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:06.160
not forget, the show didn't reveal until season
four that it was Lysa who actually poisoned

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.220
her own husband.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:12.040
OK, so what do you think about those censored
and deleted death scenes?

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:15.700
And, of all the characters we've lost over
the seasons, who do you think has had the

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:16.800
best death so far?

